The routine at2-s computes the direction of a point (x, y), in whole degrees from 0 to 359, by a polynomial of two
terms.

Input: y and x from -999 to 999, not both 0. Result: the direction of the point (x, y), measured anticlockwise
from the positive x axis, in whole degrees rounded, from 0 to 359 (a direction that rounds to 360 is 0).

Call: put x in cell 79, load y into the accumulator and JMP 12. It returns with the direction in the accumulator.
Cells 12-98 (87 cells). It writes cell 99 and, of its own cells, 77-78, 80-85, 98; nothing else.
Cost: at most 7925 instructions a call, 7140.2 on average over the domain, counted from the calling JMP to the
return jump. Worst error: 0.699 degrees from the exact value, at y = -912, x = -890; 462,024 of the 3,996,000
inputs are not the correctly rounded value.
To move the routine down by d cells, subtract d from every address in its instructions that points into
cells 12-98; addresses 00 and 99 stay as they are. It holds no address bases.

